  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 217 PM MDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nToday marks the final afternoon of tranquil, pristine weather \nacross eastern Idaho before a major autumn storm system alters \nthe regional pattern. Unseasonably mild temperatures and dry air\nwill hold through Friday, with a few localized spots actually \ntopping today's highs by a degree or two. The main change on \nFriday will be a tightening surface pressure gradient, which \nwill drive breezy southwest winds across the forecast area. Wind\ngusts of 25 to 35 mph are expected across most lower \nelevations, with gusts topping 40 mph along central mountain \nridgelines. While gusty, these speeds are anticipated to remain \nbelow advisory criteria outside of localized fire weather \nimpacts. See fire weather discussion below. \n\nA drastic synoptic transition unfolds Saturday as split flow \nestablishes itself across the western United States. Global \nguidance (GFS and ECMWF) agrees on dropping a closed upper-level\nlow south along and off the California coast before it tracks \neastward. Out ahead of this system, tropical moisture associated\nwith the remnants of Tropical Cyclone Rachel will be entrained \nnorthward directly into central and eastern Idaho.\n\nShower and thunderstorm coverage will surge Saturday and remain\nactive into early next week. This rich moisture plume will \nsupport heavy rainfall totals as PWAT values are nearing \nclimatological maximums. Current forecast calls for 1.0 to 2.0 \ninches forecast across the South Hills, Albion Mountains, and \nthe southeastern/eastern highlands. Highest ridgelines east of \nI-15 could push 2.25 to 2.50 inches. Downstream QPF spillover \nwill bring 1.0 to 1.5 inches to the Snake Plain from Pocatello \nnorthward through Rexburg. Farther west, central mountain \nvalleys will see lighter amounts ranging from 0.25 to 0.75 \ninches. While these eastern highland totals represent the upper \ntier of model ensemble blends, they remain well within the realm\nof possibility given the tropical origin of the air mass. \nBecause minor shifts in the track of Rachel's moisture axis \ncould alter localized totals, Flood or Flash Flood Watch \nissuances have been deferred to Friday's forecast cycle but stay\ntuned. \n\nColder air wrapping into the system Sunday and Monday will drive\nsnow levels down to around 7,000 feet across the central\nmountains and along the Continental Divide. While lower\nelevations will see mostly rain, several inches of wet snow are\npossible for high peaks and pass levels toward Island Park. A\nsecondary upper-level trough is forecast to drop into the\nIntermountain West during the middle of next week, reinforcing\ncold air advection and keeping temperatures well below seasonal\naverages. Frost/Freeze headlines will likely be needed for a few\ndays early next week before the \"growing season\" is officially\ndeclared over for the year.",
